<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shuja-ud-Daula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shuja-ud-Daula was Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> from 1754 to 1775 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. He fought on the Afghan side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761, then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> and Shah Alam II against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and was defeated at Buxar in 1764. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> he kept his lands but became a subsidiary ally of the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula.jpg" alt="Shuja-ud-Daula" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilly Kettle, 1735-1786, British, active in India (from 1769 to 1776) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja-ud-daula,_Nawab_of_Oudh.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
